@lesson cv-basics
Q: You have 800 labelled images for a new task. What is the best starting point?
- Train a ResNet-50 from scratch
* Fine-tune a pretrained backbone, starting with a frozen backbone and a new head
- Use k-means on raw pixels
- Train only on the 10 best images
> Pretrained features transfer well; with little data, freezing most layers avoids overfitting.

Q: Horizontal flipping is always a safe augmentation.
= false
> It depends on the invariances of the task: a flipped "6" or flipped text is no longer the same thing.

Q: When unfreezing a pretrained backbone, a common choice is to give it a learning rate that is…
* about 10× lower than the new head's
- 10× higher than the head's
- zero
- the same as training from scratch
> Pretrained weights are already good; large updates would destroy them.

Q: What does MixUp do?
- Shuffles pixels within an image
* Blends two images and their labels with the same mixing weight
- Rotates images randomly
- Removes the background
> \(\tilde x=\lambda x_i+(1-\lambda)x_j\), and the same for the one-hot labels.

@lesson detection-segmentation
Q: What is the IoU of boxes [0, 0, 10, 10] and [5, 5, 15, 15]? (three decimals)
= 0.143 ±0.002
> Intersection 25, union 175, so IoU = 25/175 ≈ 0.143.

Q: What does non-maximum suppression remove?
* Lower-confidence boxes that overlap a higher-confidence box by more than a threshold
- All boxes below 50% confidence
- Boxes at the image edge
- The background class
> Detectors predict many overlapping boxes per object; NMS keeps the best one.

Q: Why does the focal loss help one-stage detectors?
- It speeds up inference
* It down-weights the many easy background examples so training focuses on hard ones
- It removes the need for anchors
- It replaces NMS
> \(-(1-p_t)^\gamma\log p_t\) shrinks the loss of confidently correct predictions.

Q: What gives U-Net its sharp segmentation boundaries?
* Skip connections that pass high-resolution encoder features to the decoder
- A larger softmax
- Max pooling
- Removing the encoder
> Fine spatial detail lost by downsampling is recovered from the encoder.

@lesson vit-clip
Q: A 224×224 image split into 16×16 patches gives how many patch tokens?
= 196
> \((224/16)^2=14^2=196\), plus a [CLS] token in the original ViT.

Q: How does CLIP classify an image zero-shot?
- It was trained on every class name
* It embeds prompts like "a photo of a {label}" and picks the one most similar to the image embedding
- It runs a detector first
- It asks a human
> Image and text share an embedding space trained contrastively.

Q: In CLIP's training batch, what are the negatives for an image?
* The texts of the other images in the same batch
- Random noise images
- Nothing; there are no negatives
- Images of the same class
> The InfoNCE loss treats the matching caption as the correct class among all captions in the batch.

Q: ViTs need more data or stronger pretraining than CNNs mainly because…
- they have no parameters
* they lack the built-in locality and translation-equivariance biases of convolutions
- they cannot use GPUs
- attention is inaccurate
> Weaker inductive biases must be learned from data, but they scale very well when data is abundant.

@lesson video-3d
Q: In tracking-by-detection, which algorithm assigns new detections to existing tracks?
- k-means
* The Hungarian algorithm on an IoU/appearance cost matrix
- Gradient descent
- BFS
> It finds the minimum-cost one-to-one assignment; a Kalman filter predicts where each track should be.

Q: What does a NeRF learn?
* A function from 3D position and viewing direction to density and colour
- A depth map for one image
- A mesh with textures
- A 2D segmentation mask
> Pixels are rendered by integrating density and colour along camera rays.

Q: PointNet uses a symmetric max-pool so that its output does not depend on the order of the input points.
= true
> Point clouds are unordered sets, just like a graph's neighbours.

@lesson exam
Q: A detector reaches mAP@0.5 of 0.80 but mAP@[0.5:0.95] of 0.35. What does that suggest?
* It finds objects but localizes boxes imprecisely
- It misses most objects
- It has too many classes
- The metric is broken
> Strict IoU thresholds expose loose boxes; better box regression losses or higher-resolution features help.

Q: Which components does a vision-language model like LLaVA combine? (select all)
* A pretrained vision encoder
* A projector into the LLM's embedding space
* A language model decoder
- A U-Net
> Image patches become "visual tokens" that the LLM reads alongside text.

Q: You must segment tumours that occupy 1% of each scan. Which loss addition helps most with the imbalance?
- MSE
* Dice loss alongside cross-entropy
- Hinge loss
- No change
> Dice measures overlap directly and is far less dominated by the large background.

Q: A 336×336 image with 14×14 patches becomes how many visual tokens?
= 576
> \((336/14)^2=24^2=576\). High-resolution tiling multiplies this, which costs context and money.
